ely to  Challenger and I've played against some  of the players that are up at the very  top and they are super humans another  way that you can play is socially you  just want to come in hang out with your  friends spend some time it's a  completely different experience and then  there's players that kind of make their  own game within the game there's enough  flexibility for that variability to be  winning in there but even though that is  a smaller player base we still need to  make sure that we're serving that very  important player base that we have  within all of these use cases the  fundamental rules of modeling still  apply so you're still have to be very  careful and find a lot of information  within your feature engineering so that  you can accurately represent all of the  variability within your data you need to  account for bias and normalization and  there will always be missing data you  need to control for confounds if in a  particular major release we have new  content a couple of countries go on  vacation maybe we introduce a new map  and we throw in a brand new champion in  one release all of a sudden you have all  of these different confounds within the  system but you still need to be able to  model them and answer questions about  them there's going to be outliers not  just in terms of people who are trying  strange things or scripting but you'll  also have outliers in terms of  experience and you need to understand  what's going there there's always a  danger for overfitting particularly when  you have data at this scale and finally  the most important thing I think when  you're applying data science to business  use cases is adding in subject matter  needs or considerations and in this case  it means that the player experience is  fair and fun so when you're building a  model for gaming you might not want to  use the most optimal or the most  accurate model you would want the model  that provides the best experience to the  entire audience ove